1
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:19,842
In 1959, David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel lowered an electrode into the visual cortex of a cat, and moved a bar of light across its field of view.

2
00:00:19,892 --> 00:00:25,188
A single neuron answered, and its spikes were audible as clicks on tape.

3
00:00:25,238 --> 00:00:28,233
The clicks won them the 1981 Nobel Prize.

4
00:00:28,283 --> 00:00:33,950
They meant: seeing is built, cell by cell, from single features of the world.

5
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:52,496
This piece plays that recording into a chip designed by Federico Corradi: sixteen analog spiking neurons on one piece of silicon, three point six by five point two millimetres.

6
00:00:52,546 --> 00:01:02,639
Each neuron is a circuit, like this one: a capacitor holds the membrane voltage, a transistor leaks it away, and a comparator watches for the threshold.

7
00:01:02,689 --> 00:01:05,309
It has no clock and computes no numbers.

8
00:01:05,359 --> 00:01:08,112
It answers in continuous time, in physics.

9
00:01:08,162 --> 00:01:10,314
And it never answers identically.

10
00:01:10,364 --> 00:01:12,850
No two neurons, born or made, ever do.

11
00:01:17,500 --> 00:01:23,362
Every click on the tape becomes one spike, delivered to neuron fourteen through one synapse.

12
00:01:23,412 --> 00:01:29,081
This is its circuit: a few transistors that turn a digital spike into a pulse of current.

13
00:01:29,131 --> 00:01:33,130
The weight is electric charge — how much current, for how long.

14
00:01:33,180 --> 00:01:36,150
Not a number stored in memory: a physical fact.

15
00:01:38,600 --> 00:01:44,293
Here is the whole array on the die: sixteen neurons, and the synapses that wire them together.

16
00:01:44,343 --> 00:01:46,187
Sixteen neurons is not a brain.

17
00:01:46,237 --> 00:01:56,450
It is the start of a different bet: that machines which have to perceive, in real time, on the power the world can spare, should be made of physics rather than numbers.

18
00:01:59,500 --> 00:02:10,311
The visitor hears both: a cat's cortex from 1959, and a silicon neuron from 2026, in the same room, sixty-seven years apart — almost in unison.

19
00:02:10,361 --> 00:02:13,653
Those clicks are where machine vision began.

20
00:02:13,703 --> 00:02:17,450
Today's neural networks are a diagram of this cat.

